  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride may interact with CD70 antigen through a series of molecular targets. The drug is implicated in targeting the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which is a key component of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2) and is involved in epigenetic regulation. This complex is also targeted by other molecules, such as 7,10-Octadecadienoic acid, methyl ester and Glabrol, which in turn target CD70 antigen. The consistent presence of these targets across different pathways indicates a potential mechanism by which Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride may influence CD70 antigen expression or function. The evidence is supported by a high final score, suggesting a strong relationship, but the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated. The pathways highlight a possible role of Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride in modulating the activity of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which could have implications for CD70 antigen regulation.",
